    {
      "card_ids": ["C01"],
      "claim": "Saxitoxin, a guanidine-containing neurotoxin, was detected in cat brain and medulla after systemic exposure, demonstrating that guanidine-based polar molecules can achieve CNS access.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C02"],
      "claim": "Cyclocreatine, a cyclic guanidine, showed brain uptake and cognitive benefit in a brain creatine-transporter knockout mouse, supporting CNS access for guanidine-containing small molecules.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C03"],
      "claim": "AMD3100, a highly basic polyamine CXCR4 antagonist, was detected in CSF after systemic administration, indicating that even strongly charged basic molecules can have measurable CNS penetration.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C04"],
      "claim": "Hydroxyzine, a basic tertiary-amine CNS drug, readily crosses the BBB and is not subject to P-gp efflux, supporting CNS access for lipophilic basic amines.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "moderate",
  "decision_summary": "The query is a small guanidine-azepane ethylamine. Direct evidence from guanidine-containing molecules (saxitoxin, cyclocreatine) and polybasic amines (AMD3100) shows that such polar/basic structures can achieve meaningful CNS access. The query is smaller and more lipophilic than these comparators, and no contradictory BBB evidence is present.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct brain/plasma or CSF measurement is available for the exact query molecule.",
    "The query is likely dicationic at physiological pH, and its efflux-transporter profile is unknown.",
    "The saxitoxin and AMD3100 records may represent limited rather than high CNS exposure, so the degree of access is uncertain."
  ],
